          {
            "question": "What is PNG compression and how does it work?",
            "answer": "PNG compression is a lossless data compression method that reduces file size without losing any image quality or transparency information. It uses the DEFLATE algorithm combined with advanced filtering to identify and eliminate redundant data patterns, resulting in smaller file sizes while maintaining perfect image fidelity."
          },
          {
            "question": "How much can I compress a PNG file?",
            "answer": "Compression ratios vary significantly depending on image content and complexity. Simple graphics with limited colors can achieve 70-90% size reduction, while complex photographs may see 10-30% reduction. Images with transparency typically compress less than solid backgrounds due to the additional alpha channel data."
          },
          {
            "question": "Does PNG compression affect image quality or transparency?",
            "answer": "No, PNG uses lossless compression, which means the image quality, colors, and transparency information remain exactly the same after compression and decompression. Every pixel is preserved perfectly, making PNG ideal for graphics, logos, and images requiring exact reproduction."
          },
          {
            "question": "When should I use PNG instead of JPEG or other formats?",
            "answer": "Use PNG for graphics with transparency, logos, icons, screenshots with text, digital art with solid colors, and any image requiring lossless quality. JPEG is better for photographs due to higher compression ratios, while WebP offers better compression for web use but with less universal support."
          },
          {
            "question": "Can I compress PNG files with transparency and alpha channels?",
            "answer": "Yes, PNG compression preserves transparency and alpha channel information perfectly. This makes PNG the preferred format for web graphics, UI elements, logos, and any image that needs to work on different background colors or require transparency effects."
          },
          {
            "question": "How long does PNG compression take with your tool?",
            "answer": "Our PNG compressor typically processes images in under 3 seconds, even for large files up to 10MB. Processing time depends on file size, complexity, and your device's processing power. All compression happens locally in your browser for maximum privacy and speed."
          },
          {
            "question": "What are the best practices for PNG compression?",
            "answer": "Best practices include: using appropriate color depth (8-bit for simple graphics, 24-bit for photos, 32-bit for transparency), optimizing color palettes, removing unnecessary metadata, resizing to exact dimensions needed, and testing different compression settings to find the optimal balance between file size and quality."
          },
          {
            "question": "Is there a limit to the number of PNG files I can compress?",
            "answer": "Our tool supports batch processing of multiple PNG files simultaneously. While there's no strict limit, we recommend processing files in batches of 20-50 for optimal performance. Each file can be up to 10MB in size, and all processing happens locally in your browser."
          },
          {
            "question": "How does PNG compression compare to other image formats?",
            "answer": "PNG offers lossless compression with transparency support, making it ideal for graphics and web use. Compared to JPEG, PNG has larger file sizes for photos but maintains perfect quality. WebP offers better compression but less browser support. PNG remains the gold standard for graphics requiring transparency and lossless quality."
          },
          {
            "question": "Can I use the compressed PNG files commercially?",
            "answer": "Yes, all compressed PNG files maintain their original licensing and can be used for any purpose, including commercial use. Our compression process doesn't add any watermarks or restrictions to your images. The compressed files are identical in quality to the originals, just with smaller file sizes."
          }
