      "faq": {
        "title": "Preguntas Frecuentes",
        "description": "JPEG Preguntas comunes sobre",
        "items": [
          {
            "question": "What is JPEG compression and how does it work?",
            "answer": "JPEG compression is a lossy compression method that reduces file size by removing visually imperceptible details. It uses Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT) and quantization to achieve 50-90% file size reduction while maintaining good visual quality for photographs."
          },
          {
            "question": "What quality setting should I use for JPEG compression?",
            "answer": "For web images, use 80-90% quality for optimal balance. For print materials, use 90-95%. For thumbnails or previews, 60-80% is sufficient. Higher quality means larger files but better visual fidelity."
          },
          {
            "question": "Is JPEG compression lossless or lossy?",
            "answer": "Standard JPEG uses lossy compression, meaning some image data is permanently discarded to reduce file size. While lossless JPEG variants exist, they're rarely used because they don't provide significant compression benefits over PNG."
          },
          {
            "question": "When should I use JPEG instead of PNG?",
            "answer": "Use JPEG for photographs, realistic images, and complex scenes with many colors. Use PNG for graphics, logos, screenshots, and images requiring transparency or perfect pixel-level accuracy. JPEG typically provides 10x better compression for photos."
          },
          {
            "question": "What is progressive JPEG and should I use it?",
            "answer": "Progressive JPEG loads images in multiple passes, showing a low-resolution version first that gradually improves. This provides better perceived loading speed on slower connections and is recommended for web images larger than 10KB."
          },
          {
            "question": "Does JPEG support transparency?",
            "answer": "No, JPEG doesn't support transparency or alpha channels. If you need transparency, use PNG for lossless or WebP for lossy compression with transparency support."
          },
          {
            "question": "How much can I compress a JPEG without losing quality?",
            "answer": "You can typically compress JPEG by 50-70% with minimal visible quality loss using 85-90% quality settings. Beyond that, artifacts may become noticeable depending on the image content and viewing conditions."
          },
          {
            "question": "Will compressing an already compressed JPEG reduce quality further?",
            "answer": "Yes, each time you compress an already-compressed JPEG, you introduce additional quality loss (generation loss). Always work from the original uncompressed image when possible to maintain best quality."
          },
          {
            "question": "Can I batch compress multiple JPEG files?",
            "answer": "Yes, our tool supports batch processing of multiple JPEG files simultaneously. You can compress up to 10 files at once with consistent quality settings, saving time while maintaining uniform results."
          },
          {
            "question": "Is my data safe when using this online compressor?",
            "answer": "Absolutely! All compression happens locally in your browser using JavaScript. Your images never leave your device or get uploaded to our servers, ensuring complete privacy and security."
          }
        ]
      },
