        "items": [
          {
            "question": "What's the difference between GIF and other formats?",
            "answer": "GIF supports animation and transparency, making it ideal for simple graphics with limited colors and animated content. Unlike JPEG, it uses lossless compression for solid colors, and unlike PNG, it supports frame-based animation."
          },
          {
            "question": "How much can GIF files be compressed?",
            "answer": "GIF compression depends on image complexity. Simple graphics with limited colors can achieve 50-80% size reduction, while complex images or photographs may only see 20-40% reduction due to the 256-color limitation."
          },
          {
            "question": "Should I use GIF for photos?",
            "answer": "GIF is not ideal for photos due to its 256-color limit, which can result in significant quality loss and color banding. Use JPEG for photographs or modern formats like WebP/AVIF for better compression with full color support."
          },
          {
            "question": "How do I optimize GIF animations?",
            "answer": "Optimize GIF animations by reducing the color palette, adjusting frame rate, removing unnecessary frames, using frame disposal methods efficiently, and considering the animation's purpose and duration. Keep animations short and simple for best results."
          }
        ]
